<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Khasso</h1><p class="st-meta">1681 CE – 1880 CE (approx.) · capital Medine</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Fulbe-ruled kingdom on the upper Senegal river around Medine, a key ally of the French during the siege of 1857.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Khasso arose in the late 17th century when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulbe</a> herders who had settled among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Malinke</a> farmers on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> created a kingdom. Its rulers came from the Diallo clan, and the population, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khassonke">Khassonke</a>, mixed Fulbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mande-peoples">Mande</a> culture and language. It lay between more powerful neighbours: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bundu">Bundu</a> to the west and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaarta">Kingdom of Kaarta</a> to the east.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Khasso was a loose kingdom of related chiefdoms, often weakened by succession disputes. Its position on the river, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felou-falls">Felou</a> falls just above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medine-mali">Medine</a> at the head of navigation, made it a stage on the trade route between the coast and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, and its rulers dealt with French traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a>. Most of the population kept traditional religion alongside a Muslim minority.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1855 the French built a fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medine-mali">Medine</a> with the support of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juka-sambala">Juka Sambala</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-hadj-umar-tall">Umar Tall</a> besieged it in 1857, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-holle">Paul Holl</a> and Sambala held out until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Faidherbe</a> arrived. Khasso became a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> around 1880 and was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-khasso.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Khasso" width="320" height="170"><figcaption>Image: ? localisation en jaune par Ji-Elle 15:18, 28 March 2008 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KhassoMap.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1681 CE – 1880 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Medine</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Juka Sambala</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion and Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khassonke</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mali">Mali</a>, <a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khasso, Xaaso</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1780&amp;focus=kingdom-of-khasso" aria-label="Kingdom of Khasso on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4626 1477 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Khasso lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4946 1733h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4946 1733h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Mali. <a href="/atlas?year=1780&amp;focus=kingdom-of-khasso">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

